    "body": "Copper cathode production in electrowinning plant. ( Stock image by Jorge. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie October 16, 2026\nThe global refined copper surplus is expected to more than triple next year as production growth outpaces consumption, despite demand from electrification, artificial intelligence and data centres, the International Copper Study Group says.\nThe surplus is forecast to reach 368,000 tonnes in 2027, up from 104,000 tonnes this year, the Lisbon-based intergovernmental group said in its October forecast . Next year’s excess would represent about 1.3% of global consumption, with refined output of 29.7 million tonnes against usage of 29.3 million tonnes.\nRefined production growth is expected to accelerate to 3.4% in 2027 from just 0.4% this year, exceeding projected consumption growth of 2.5%. The rebound would be supported by smelter and refinery ramp-ups in Indonesia, India and the European Union, improved operating rates in Chile and increased production from scrap.\nThe outlook points to a widening surplus of refined metal even as disruptions at major mines restrict supplies of copper concentrate used by smelters. Recovering mine output and growing scrap production are expected to help ease those constraints next year.\nThe 2026 surplus estimate is slightly higher than the 96,000 tonnes projected in April, while next year’s forecast is broadly in line with the earlier outlook.\nMine supply recovery\nWorld copper mine production is expected to grow by 1.3% this year, down from the 1.6% projected in April following downward revisions to output in Chile, China and Indonesia.\nGrowth is being supported by ramp-ups at Rio Tinto’s (LSE, NYSE, ASX: RIO) Oyu Tolgoi mine in Mongolia, Amman Mineral Internasional’s (IDX: AMMN) Batu Hijau mine in Indonesia and privately held Russian Copper’s Malmyz mine in Russia.\nExpansions at Zijin Mining’s (HKEX: 2899; SSE: 601899) Julong mine in China and Ural Mining and Metallurgical’s Svyatogor operations in Russia are also contributing, alongside higher output at Peru’s Antamina, jointly owned by BHP (LSE, NYSE, ASX: BHP), Glencore (LSE: GLEN), Teck Resources (TSX: TECK.B; NYSE: TECK) and Mitsubishi (TSE: 8058), and processing of stockpiled ore at First Quantum Minerals’ (TSX: FM) Cobre Panama.\nProduction remains constrained at Freeport-McMoRan’s (NYSE: FCX) Grasberg mine in Indonesia and Kamoa-Kakula in the Democratic Republic of Congo, jointly owned by Ivanhoe Mines (TSX: IVN; US-OTC: IVPAF), Zijin Mining and other partners, following incidents in 2025. Declining Chilean mine output is also limiting global growth.\nRefined copper production from scrap is forecast to increase by about 4.7% in 2027, while primary refined output from mined copper is expected to rise by around 3%.\nDemand growth lags\nGlobal refined copper usage is projected to increase by 1.5% this year and 2.5% next year, with Asia remaining the main source of growth.\nChina, which accounts for 59% of global refined copper consumption, is expected to increase apparent usage by 2.5% this year, while usage across the rest of the world remains essentially unchanged.\nThe Chinese calculation uses reported production, net trade and changes in Shanghai Futures Exchange stocks. It excludes changes in unreported inventories held by state agencies, producers, consumers and traders, which can materially alter the global supply-demand balance, the group cautioned.\nThe ICSG lowered its 2026 consumption growth outlook slightly from April, citing weaker expectations for the Middle East, North America and the European Union. Demand in Japan is also expected to remain subdued.\nConsumption growth next year should benefit from an anticipated improvement in global economic activity, new and expanded plants making semi-finished copper products such as wire, tube and sheet, and stronger operating rates at those plants in the Middle East.\nThe forecasts remain vulnerable to supply disruptions and geopolitical developments, particularly the conflict in the Middle East, which could affect both production and consumption, the group said.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Africa Australia, NZ & South Pacific Canada South America United States\nCOMPANY: Amman Mineral Internasional First Quantum Minerals Freeport-McMoRan Inc Glencore Ivanhoe Mines Mitsubishi Rio Tinto Teck Resources Zijin Mining\nRepublish this article",
